# Claude Rules for Minimal Text Editor Project

## Development Patterns

### Component Architecture
- Use functional components with hooks exclusively
- Implement manager pattern for business logic (DocumentManager, StorageManager)
- Keep components focused on UI concerns, delegate logic to managers
- Use TypeScript strictly - no `any` types, proper interface definitions

### Lexical Integration
- Custom plugin approach required (no third-party Lexical plugins)
- AutocompleteNode extends TextNode for non-editable entries
- Plugin composition pattern in EditorWithSync component
- Preserve editor state through document switches

### Storage & Persistence
- IndexedDB via abstraction layers (IndexedDBHelper → StorageManager → DocumentManager)
- Debounced auto-save (2-second delay) for optimal UX
- Proper error handling for storage quota and connection issues
- Maintain document schema compatibility across updates

### Testing Standards
- 100% test pass rate requirement
- React Testing Library for component testing
- Custom Lexical testing utilities for editor behavior
- Mock IndexedDB for storage operation testing
- Test both happy path and edge cases

### UI/UX Patterns
- Minimal, flat design aesthetic throughout
- Responsive design with mobile considerations
- Loading states for all async operations
- Consistent hover/focus states and transitions
- Proper keyboard navigation support

## Code Quality Standards

### File Organization
```
src/
├── components/          # UI components
├── editor/             
│   ├── nodes/          # Custom Lexical nodes
│   └── plugins/        # Custom Lexical plugins
├── hooks/              # Reusable React hooks
├── types/              # TypeScript definitions
├── utils/              # Business logic managers
└── __tests__/          # Test files
```

### Naming Conventions
- PascalCase for components and classes
- camelCase for functions, variables, and methods
- UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- Descriptive names that indicate purpose
- Manager suffix for business logic classes

### State Management
- Local state with useState for UI concerns
- useCallback for event handlers to prevent re-renders
- useRef for DOM references and mutable values
- No global state library needed - prop drilling acceptable for current scope

### Error Handling
- Try-catch blocks for all async operations
- Graceful degradation for storage failures
- User-friendly error messages in UI
- Console logging for debugging in development

## Project-Specific Rules

### Global Brain Integration
- Flat list design for sidebar content (no card-based styling)
- Click-to-load workflow creates full documents
- Maintain existing document data structure when loading Global Brain items
- Loading states with spinner feedback during async operations

### Autocomplete Behavior
- Trigger on `<>` characters only
- Dynamic filtering based on user input after trigger
- Keyboard navigation: arrows for selection, enter/tab to accept
- Single backspace to delete completed entries
- Visual distinction with blue background for completed entries

### Document Management
- Auto-save with visual indicators
- Recent documents sorted by modification date
- Word count tracking and display
- Export/import in JSON format with metadata preservation

### Performance Requirements
- Autocomplete response time <100ms
- Document save operations <500ms
- Smooth sidebar resizing at 60fps
- Test suite execution <10 seconds

## Development Workflow

### Git Practices
- Conventional commits with clear descriptions
- Feature branch development
- Commit frequently with logical changesets
- Detailed commit messages explaining the why, not just what

### Build & Development
- Vite for fast development server with HMR
- TypeScript strict mode enabled
- Jest for testing with jsdom environment
- ESLint for code quality (when configured)

### Testing Approach
- Test-driven development preferred
- Unit tests for individual components
- Integration tests for workflows
- Mock external dependencies (IndexedDB, File API)
- Test user interactions with Testing Library utilities

## Maintenance Notes

### Dependencies
- Keep React/Lexical versions aligned
- Update dependencies cautiously to avoid breaking changes  
- Prefer stable, well-maintained packages
- Document any critical dependency decisions

### Documentation
- Memory bank files updated after significant changes
- Component interfaces properly documented with TypeScript
- README kept current with setup instructions
- Code comments for complex business logic only

### Future Considerations
- Global Brain dataset expansion planned
- Autocomplete metadata integration roadmap defined  
- UI polish phase identified
- Performance monitoring capabilities if needed